<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Beylik</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as beyliks</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A beylik was a Turkic principality ruled by a bey, especially one of the small states that emerged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> after the collapse of Seljuk authority in the thirteenth century. One of these, the beylik of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osman-i">Osman I</a> in the Bithynian borderlands near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bursa">Bursa</a>, lay on the frontier with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>. It grew into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The state seized religious endowments, former <b>beylik</b> lands and the property of rebels, above all after the Mokrani revolt, and the Warnier law of 1873 opened collectively held tribal land to sale.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…fractured political landscape of north-western Anatolia in the late thirteenth century, where the collapse of Seljuk authority following the Mongol victory at the Battle of Köse Dağ in 1243 had fragmented Asia Minor into a constellation of competing Turkic <b>beyliks</b>, petty principalities established along the shrinking Byzantine frontier.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Turkish principalities, or <b>beyliks</b>, that emerged from its collapse, including the Karamanids and the Ottomans, inherited its institutions, artistic models and frontier culture, and Ottoman tradition later claimed that the Seljuk sultan had granted Osman’s family their first lands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…weakened, a diminished state later ruled by the Sayyid and Lodi dynasties until the Mughal conquest; the campaigns in Persia and Iraq depopulated agricultural regions that had supported dense populations for millennia; and the victory at Ankara temporarily relieved Byzantium, Serbia and the Anatolian <b>beyliks</b> of Ottoman pressure.</blockquote><a href="/empire/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its oldest stage, Old Ottoman Turkish, used until the 16th century, was almost identical with the Turkish of the Seljuk empire and the Anatolian <b>beyliks</b>, and is often counted as part of Old Anatolian Turkish.</blockquote><a href="/language/ottoman-turkish">Ottoman Turkish · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Roger de Flor, commander of the Catalan Company, mercenaries hired by Byzantine Emperor Andronikos II Palaiologos against the Anatolian <b>beyliks</b>, was murdered.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-30">30 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
